      "content": "Proponents of the \"race realism\" movement attribute IQ score differences among racial groups to biological factors, specifically hereditary influences. They argue that these differences in IQ scores cannot be solely attributed to environmental or socioeconomic factors, and suggest that cultural and genetic adaptations have led to varying levels of genetic \"fitness\" within each group. Some proponents cite the work of eugenicist, William Shockley, who claimed that the timing of IQ tests are biased against certain racial groups, resulting in artificially low IQ scores. They also argue that the Flynn Effect, a polynomial rise in IQ scores over time, ignores this fact and instead attributed to the spread of intelligence through based variables in human population rather than proper environmental factors.",
